{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789832183,"nonce":"JldP1zHs3FRq9XnvVLRnNosS+W4=","title":"integer literals: 0xFFFFFFFF reads as 4294967295, and `9i64` is an Int","message":"Haxe reads an integer literal through Int32.of_string: a hex, binary or octal literal that fits 32 bits is an `Int` with those bits (`0xFFFFFFFF` is -1, `0x80000000` is -2147483648), and a literal that does not fit `Int` -- a decimal past 2147483647 or anything past 32 bits -- is a `Float`. Both parsers kept the literal as its i64 value, so `eq(a.low, 0xFFFFFFFF)` compared -1 against 4294967295 (11 of TestInt64's 12 FAILCHECKs) and `0x7FFFFFFF + 1` did not wrap.\n\nThe Haxe 4.3 suffixes were lexed and dropped, so `0x7FFFFFFFFFFFFFFFi64` became an Int that `Int64.ofInt` truncated to 0x7FFFFFFF.\n\nFix: one `int_literal_kind` shared by the RD and legacy parsers -- wrap non-decimal within 32 bits, Float past Int, `-2147483648` folded to the Int, and an `i64` suffix desugared to `haxe.Int64.make(high, low)`.","files":null}]}